                "rationale": "Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP) is an infection of ascitic fluid without an apparent intra-abdominal surgically treatable source, typically caused by bacterial translocation of enteric gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae). The diagnostic gold standard is an ABSOLUTE NEUTROPHIL / PMN COUNT >= 250 cells/mm3 (calculated as total WBC count x % neutrophils). Treatment must NOT await culture results: empirical therapy of choice is a third-generation cephalosporin such as IV Cefotaxime (2 g every 8 hours) or IV Ceftriaxone, combined with IV Albumin (1.5 g/kg on day 1, 1 g/kg on day 3) to prevent hepatorenal syndrome and reduce mortality.",
