              "question": "A 28-year-old female presents with chronic watery diarrhea, bloating, weight loss, iron deficiency anemia refractory to oral iron, and an intensely pruritic vesicular rash over her extensor elbows and knees (Dermatitis Herpetiformis). What is the most sensitive and specific serological screening test, and what is the Marsh classification finding on duodenal biopsy?",
              "options": {
                "A": "IgA Anti-Tissue Transglutaminase (anti-tTG) and Anti-Endomysial antibodies (EMA); Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes (Marsh Type 3)",
                "B": "Anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies; Normal mucosa",
                "C": "Serum gastrin level; Peptic ulceration",
                "D": "Anti-mitochondrial antibodies; Granulomatous bile duct destruction"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Celiac Disease (Gluten-Sensitive Enteropathy) is an autoimmune enteropathy triggered by ingestion of gluten (gliadin fraction found in wheat, barley, rye) in individuals carrying HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8. Hallmarks: Malabsorption, iron-deficiency anemia, and Dermatitis Herpetiformis (granular IgA deposits in dermal papillae). Gold-standard serology: Serum IgA Anti-Tissue Transglutaminase (anti-tTG) and IgA Anti-Endomysial Antibodies (EMA - > 98% specificity). (Must check total IgA to rule out selective IgA deficiency!). Duodenal biopsy (Marsh III): Intraepithelial lymphocytosis (> 25/100 enterocytes), crypt hyperplasia, and total villous blunting/atrophy. Definitive treatment: Lifelong strict Gluten-Free Diet.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "CORRECT: Celiac Disease (Gluten-Sensitive Enteropathy) is an autoimmune enteropathy triggered by ingestion of gluten (gliadin fraction found in wheat, barley, rye) in individuals carrying HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8. Hallmarks: Malabsorption, iron-deficiency anemia, and Dermatitis Herpetiformis (granular IgA deposits in dermal papillae). Gold-standard serology: Serum IgA Anti-Tissue Transglutaminase (anti-tTG) and IgA Anti-Endomysial Antibodies (EMA - > 98% specificity). (Must check total IgA to rule out selective IgA deficiency!). Duodenal biopsy (Marsh III): Intraepithelial lymphocytosis (> 25/100 enterocytes), crypt hyperplasia, and total villous blunting/atrophy. Definitive treatment: Lifelong strict Gluten-Free Diet.",
                  "B": "INCORRECT: Anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies; Normal mucosa is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "C": "INCORRECT: Serum gastrin level; Peptic ulceration is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "D": "INCORRECT: Anti-mitochondrial antibodies; Granulomatous bile duct destruction is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Celiac Disease: HLA-DQ2 / HLA-DQ8. Serology: IgA anti-tTG (first-line) and IgA anti-Endomysial (EMA). Duodenal Biopsy: Villous atrophy + Crypt hyperplasia + Intraepithelial lymphocytosis (Marsh III). Extraintestinal clue: Dermatitis Herpetiformis (treat with Dapsone + gluten-free diet). Feared malignancy of untreated celiac = Enteropathy-Associated T-Cell Lymphoma (EATL)."
              },
              "tags": [
                "MED",
                "Celiac Disease Serology & Histopathology",
                "Unit 4",
                "High Yield Practice"
              ]
