              "topic": "Barrett's Esophagus & Adenocarcinoma",
              "question": "A 54-year-old male with a 15-year history of chronic GERD undergoes screening upper endoscopy. The squamocolumnar junction (Z-line) is displaced 4 cm proximal to the gastroesophageal junction, with salmon-pink velvety mucosa extending into the tubular esophagus. Histopathology demonstrates specialized intestinal metaplasia. What specific cellular feature defines intestinal metaplasia in Barrett's esophagus?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Presence of intestinal-type Goblet Cells with acidic mucin (staining with Alcian blue at pH 2.5)",
                "B": "Stratified non-keratinizing squamous cells",
                "C": "Gastric foveolar cells alone",
                "D": "Chief cells secreting pepsinogen"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
