              "id": "MED_ENDO_002",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2023 Recall",
              "topic": "Diabetic Emergencies - DKA Protocol",
              "question": "A 19-year-old female with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus presents to the triage with deep, rapid respirations (Kussmaul breathing), abdominal pain, nausea, and a fruity odor on her breath. Laboratory findings:\n• Plasma Glucose: 480 mg/dL\n• Arterial Blood Gas: pH 7.15, HCO3- 9 mEq/L, PaCO2 24 mmHg\n• Serum Sodium: 136 mEq/L | Serum Potassium: 3.1 mEq/L\n• Urine Ketones: 4+ (strongly positive)\nWhat is the mandatory first step before initiating intravenous insulin infusion?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Administer IV regular insulin bolus 0.14 U/kg immediately",
                "B": "Infuse 0.9% Normal Saline and replace Potassium to raise serum K+ > 3.3 mEq/L before starting insulin",
                "C": "Administer IV Sodium Bicarbonate bolus stat",
                "D": "Start subcutaneous Glargine insulin"
              },
              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "This patient has severe Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA). While insulin is the definitive hormone required to halt ketogenesis, INSULIN DRIVES POTASSIUM FROM THE EXTRACELLULAR FLUID INTO CELLS. If insulin is administered when serum potassium is < 3.3 mEq/L, it can precipitate catastrophic hypokalemia, causing lethal ventricular arrhythmias (ventricular fibrillation, asystole) and respiratory muscle paralysis. Therefore, the absolute first step is aggressive fluid resuscitation with 0.9% Normal Saline AND intravenous potassium chloride supplementation. Insulin MUST BE WITHHELD until serum potassium is restored to > 3.3 mEq/L.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Starting insulin when K+ < 3.3 mEq/L can cause fatal cardiac arrest due to sudden hypokalemia.",
                  "C": "Routine sodium bicarbonate in DKA is contraindicated because it paradoxically worsens intracellular and CSF acidosis (paradoxical CSF acidosis) and delays ketone clearance; reserved only if pH < 6.9.",
                  "D": "Subcutaneous long-acting insulin is completely inappropriate for acute resuscitation of severe DKA."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "DKA Potassium Management Rules:\n• Serum K+ < 3.3 mEq/L: HOLD insulin! Give IV KCl (20-40 mEq/hr) until K+ > 3.3 mEq/L.\n• Serum K+ 3.3 - 5.2 mEq/L: Start insulin infusion (0.1 U/kg/hr) AND add 20-30 mEq KCl per liter of IV fluid to maintain K+ between 4-5 mEq/L.\n• Serum K+ > 5.2 mEq/L: Start insulin, withhold potassium, check K+ every 2 hours.\n• Add 5% Dextrose when plasma glucose reaches 200-250 mg/dL while continuing insulin until ketoacidosis resolves (anion gap normalizes and HCO3- >= 18 mEq/L)."
              },
