                "rationale": "Primary Adrenal Insufficiency (Addison's Disease) is caused by autoimmune destruction of all three zones of the adrenal cortex (80-90% in developed countries; Tuberculosis is the commonest cause in developing countries like India). Lack of mineralocorticoids (aldosterone) causes hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and volume depletion (salt craving, orthostatic hypotension). Lack of glucocorticoids (cortisol) causes fatigue, hypoglycemia, and uninhibited feedback elevation of ACTH and POMC, producing diffuse skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation (melanocyte-stimulating hormone / MSH activity). Gold-standard diagnostic confirmation: Standard High-Dose Cosyntropin (ACTH) Stimulation Test: baseline cortisol is measured, 250 mcg IV/IM Cosyntropin is administered; normal response is peak serum cortisol > 18-20 mcg/dL at 30-60 mins. Non-response confirms adrenal insufficiency.",
