                "rationale": "SIADH is characterized by uninhibited, autonomous release of Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH / Vasopressin), leading to water retention, dilutional hyponatremia, and secondary natriuresis. Diagnostic criteria: (1) Hypotonic hyponatremia (serum Na < 135, Posm < 275), (2) Inappropriately high urine osmolality (> 100 mOsm/kg, usually > Posm), (3) High urine sodium (> 20-40 mEq/L), (4) Clinical EUVOLEMIA (no peripheral edema, normal JVP, normal skin turgor), and (5) Normal renal, thyroid, and adrenal function. Treatment of choice for asymptomatic/mild SIADH: Free Fluid Restriction (500-1000 mL/day). For severe symptomatic hyponatremia (seizures, coma): 3% Hypertonic Saline bolus (100 mL IV over 10 mins). CRITICAL RULE: Rate of correction must NEVER exceed 8 to 10 mEq/L in 24 hours (or 18 mEq/L in 48 hours) to prevent fatal OSMOTIC DEMYELINATION SYNDROME (Central Pontine Myelinolysis -> spastic quadriparesis, pseudobulbar palsy, 'locked-in' syndrome).",
