              "id": "MED_NEPH_002",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG 2024 Recall",
              "topic": "Electrolyte Emergencies - Severe Hyperkalemia",
              "question": "A 55-year-old male with End-Stage Renal Disease on maintenance hemodialysis misses two consecutive dialysis sessions. He presents with severe generalized muscle weakness and tingling. Serum Potassium is 7.4 mEq/L. The 12-lead ECG demonstrates tall, peaked, narrow T waves with loss of P waves and marked QRS widening approaching a sine-wave pattern. What is the immediate first-line medication that must be infused STAT?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Intravenous Calcium Gluconate (10%)",
                "B": "Intravenous Regular Insulin with 25% Dextrose",
                "C": "Nebulized Salbutamol",
                "D": "Oral Sodium Polystyrene Sulfonate (Kayexalate)"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Severe hyperkalemia with ECG changes (tall peaked T waves, absent P waves, wide QRS, sine wave) is a hyper-acute cardiac emergency with imminent risk of ventricular fibrillation or asystole. The IMMEDIATE FIRST-LINE INTERVENTION is 10 mL of 10% Intravenous Calcium Gluconate (or calcium chloride via central line) infused over 2-3 minutes. Calcium DOES NOT LOWER SERUM POTASSIUM LEVELS; rather, it directly antagonizes the membrane actions of hyperkalemia by restoring the normal resting membrane threshold potential, instantly STABILIZING THE CARDIAC MYOCYTE MEMBRANE within 1-3 minutes. Once the membrane is stabilized, shift agents (insulin + glucose, beta-agonists) and eliminating agents (dialysis, loop diuretics) are instituted.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "B": "Insulin + Dextrose shifts potassium into cells, but takes 15-30 minutes to act. Administering it first leaves the unstable myocardium vulnerable to sudden arrest.",
                  "C": "Nebulized salbutamol shifts potassium intracellularly over 30 minutes, serving as an adjunct.",
                  "D": "Sodium Polystyrene Sulfonate / Patiromer are intestinal potassium-binding resins that take several hours to days to eliminate potassium; completely useless in hyper-acute emergencies."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Stepwise Management of Hyperkalemic Emergency:\n1. Membrane Stabilization: IV Calcium Gluconate 10% (Immediate onset, lasts 30-60 mins).\n2. Intracellular Shift (15-30 mins onset):\n   - IV Regular Insulin 10 units + 50 mL 50% Dextrose\n   - Nebulized Salbutamol 10-20 mg\n   - IV Sodium Bicarbonate (if severe metabolic acidosis co-exists)\n3. Potassium Elimination:\n   - Emergent Hemodialysis (most effective)\n   - IV Furosemide (if residual renal function)\n   - GI binders (Patiromer, Sodium Zirconium Cyclosilicate)."
              },
