              "id": "INI_MED_RHEUM_001",
              "exam_source": "INI-CET Nov 2023 Recall",
              "topic": "Autoimmune Hematology - Immune Thrombocytopenia (ITP)",
              "question": "A 28-year-old female presents with multiple petechiae over her lower limbs and minor mucosal gingival bleeding. Complete blood count reveals:\n• Platelet count: 18,000 /uL\n• Hemoglobin: 12.8 g/dL | WBC count: 6,400 /uL\n• Peripheral blood smear shows reduced platelets with occasional giant/large platelets, without schistocytes or blasts.\nBone marrow biopsy shows normal to increased megakaryocytes with normal erythroid and myeloid maturation. What is the standard first-line pharmacological treatment, and which novel second-line class of drugs acts by stimulating the thrombopoietin receptor (TPO-R)?",
              "options": {
                "A": "First line: Platelet transfusion; Second line: Hydroxyurea",
                "B": "First line: Systemic Corticosteroids (Prednisolone / Dexamethasone) +/- IVIG; Second line: Thrombopoietin Receptor Agonists (Eltrombopag / Romiplostim)",
                "C": "First line: Splenectomy; Second line: Cyclophosphamide",
                "D": "First line: Therapeutic Plasma Exchange; Second line: Rituximab"
              },
              "correct_option": "B",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "The presentation is classic for Primary Immune Thrombocytopenia (ITP), an autoimmune disorder where autoantibodies (primarily IgG against GPIIb/IIIa and GPIb/IX) cause Fc-receptor-mediated platelet destruction by splenic macrophages. Bone marrow shows compensatory megakaryocytosis. First-line therapy for symptomatic patients or platelets < 30,000 /uL is SYSTEMIC CORTICOSTEROIDS (Oral Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day or high-dose Dexamethasone 40 mg/day for 4 days) +/- IVIG (if urgent platelet rise is required). In refractory or persistent ITP, THROMBOPOIETIN RECEPTOR AGONISTS (TPO-RAs: Eltrombopag [oral], Romiplostim [subcutaneous], Avatrombopag) stimulate the c-Mpl / TPO receptor on bone marrow megakaryocytes, markedly increasing platelet production.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "Routine prophylactic platelet transfusion is contraindicated in ITP because transfused platelets are rapidly destroyed by circulating autoantibodies; reserved strictly for life-threatening bleeding (e.g., intracranial hemorrhage).",
                  "C": "Splenectomy is a second/third-line option, never first-line.",
                  "D": "Plasma exchange is the life-saving treatment for Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP - ADAMTS13 deficiency), NOT for ITP."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "ITP Clinical Pearls for INI-CET:\n• Pathophysiology: Anti-GPIIb/IIIa antibodies + splenic platelet destruction + impaired thrombopoiesis.\n• First-line Therapy: Corticosteroids (Prednisone / Dexamethasone) +/- IVIG.\n• Second-line Therapy: TPO-RAs (Eltrombopag, Romiplostim), Rituximab (anti-CD20), or Splenectomy.\n• Caution: Eltrombopag must be taken on an empty stomach (chelated by polyvalent cations in food/dairy)."
              },
