                "rationale": "Necrotizing Fasciitis (Type 1: polymicrobial anaerobic + aerobic; Type 2: monomicrobial Group A Streptococcus [Streptococcus pyogenes / 'flesh-eating bacterium'] +/- S. aureus; Fournier's Gangrene: necrotizing infection of perineum and scrotum) is a surgical emergency with mortality > 30-50%. Cardinal clinical sign: SEVERE EXCRUCIATING PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to initial mild erythema, followed by woody-hard edema, skin bullae with dishwater-fluid discharge, dermal anesthesia (due to thrombosis of cutaneous nerves in necrotic fascia), and subcutaneous gas/crepitus. The LRINEC (Laboratory Risk Indicator for Necrotizing Fasciitis) score uses CRP (> 150), WBC, Hemoglobin, Sodium, Creatinine, and Glucose. THE ABSOLUTE FIRST-LINE LIFE-SAVING INTERVENTION IS IMMEDIATE RADICAL SURGICAL DEBRIDEMENT of all necrotic fascia until healthy, bleeding tissue is encountered. Antibiotics are essential adjuvants, but NEVER substitute for immediate surgical source control!",
