                "rationale": "Volvulus of the Colon (torsion/twisting of a mobile loop of bowel around its mesenteric axis): (1) Sigmoid Volvulus (75-80% of all volvulus): Occurs in elderly, neuropsychiatric, or institutionalized patients with chronic constipation and a redundant, elongated sigmoid colon with a narrow mesenteric base. Radiographic Hallmarks: 'COFFEE-BEAN SIGN' / 'Bent Inner Tube' sign: huge ahaustral loop pointing from left lower quadrant toward the RIGHT UPPER QUADRANT, with a 'Bird's beak' tapering on Gastrografin / barium enema. Initial Management (if no fever, peritonitis, or gangrene): RIGID / FLEXIBLE SIGMOIDOSCOPY (or colonoscopy) with gentle derotation and placement of a flatus/rectal tube (achieves immediate dramatic decompression with gush of flatus/liquid feces in > 80%). Because recurrence exceeds 50-60%, planned elective semi-urgent Sigmoid Colectomy is performed during the same admission. (2) Cecal Volvulus (15-20%): Axial torsion of mobile cecum; occurs in younger patients with unattached cecum; X-ray shows dilated comma-shaped loop oriented toward the LEFT UPPER QUADRANT; requires surgical resection (Right Hemicolectomy).",
