                "rationale": "Femoral Hernia accounts for 3% of all groin hernias, but has a 4:1 female predominance (due to wider female pelvis and multiparity). It enters the Femoral Ring into the Femoral Canal (the medial compartment of the femoral sheath, normally containing fat and the Cloquet's / Rosenmuller's lymph node). Crucial Clinical Distinctions: (1) Position: Femoral hernia neck is located INFERIOR and LATERAL to the Pubic Tubercle (Inguinal hernia neck is Superior and Medial to the pubic tubercle!). (2) Boundaries of Femoral Ring: Anterior = Inguinal (Poupart) ligament; Posterior = Pectineal (Cooper) ligament and pectineus muscle; Medial = Sharp, rigid, crescentic border of Lacunar (Gimbernat) ligament; Lateral = Femoral Vein (separated by a fibrous septum). (3) Strangulation Risk: Because the neck of the femoral canal is narrow and encircled by rigid, unyielding fibrous ligaments (lacunar and pectineal ligaments), femoral hernias have the HIGHEST RISK OF STRANGULATION of all abdominal wall hernias (30-40% present with acute strangulation!). Therefore, ALL femoral hernias mandate PROMPT SURGICAL REPAIR upon diagnosis (Low approach: McEvedy, Lotheissen, or Lockwood).",
