                "rationale": "Anatomy of the Hepatobiliary Triangle (Calot's Triangle): (1) Original Triangle of Calot (1891): Cystic duct, Common hepatic duct, and Cystic artery; (2) Modern Surgical / Hepatobiliary Triangle: Inferior border = Inferior surface of Liver (Segment V); Medial border = Common Hepatic Duct (CHD); Inferior / Lateral border = Cystic Duct. Contents: CYSTIC ARTERY (usually a branch of the Right Hepatic Artery), Lund's Lymph Node (Cystic node / Mascagni's node - reliably located directly over the cystic artery and serves as an important dissection landmark), autonomic nerve fibers, and variable aberrant bile ducts (Ducts of Luschka). Critical View of Safety (CVS / Strasberg): (1) Clearance of the hepatocystic triangle of all fat and fibrous tissue, (2) Dissection of the lower 1/3 of the gallbladder off the cystic plate of the liver bed, and (3) Visualizing EXCLUSIVELY TWO AND ONLY TWO STRUCTURES (Cystic Duct and Cystic Artery) entering the gallbladder. Achieving CVS prevents catastrophic transection of the Common Bile Duct!",
