              "question": "A 56-year-old female presents with severe right upper quadrant pain, high fever with shaking chills (spiking rigor), and fluctuating jaundice. On examination, she is hypotensive (BP 82/54 mmHg) and lethargic/confused. Laboratory tests reveal total bilirubin of 7.2 mg/dL, ALP of 680 IU/L, and WBC of 22,000/uL. What is the diagnosis, and what is the immediate life-saving procedure of choice?",
