              "id": "OBGY_PRAC_U3_20",
              "question_type": "PRACTICE",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG High-Yield Practice (NBE / AIIMS Pattern)",
              "topic": "Bartholin Abscess - Marsupialization vs Excision",
              "question": "A 26-year-old female presents with severe, throbbing unilateral vulvar pain, difficulty walking, and a tender, fluctuant, golf-ball-sized 4 cm swelling in the posterior lateral aspect of the introitus at the 5 o'clock position. What is the surgical procedure of choice to drain the abscess while preserving glandular secretory function?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Marsupialization (or Word Catheter placement)",
                "B": "Complete surgical excision of the gland in the acute setting",
                "C": "Simple needle aspiration",
                "D": "Topical antibiotic ointment alone"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "Bartholin Glands (Greater Vestibular Glands) are paired mucus-secreting compound racemose glands located in the superficial perineal pouch at the 4 and 8 o'clock positions posterolaterally to the vaginal orifice. Obstruction of the Bartholin duct (due to inspissated mucus or infection: E. coli, Bacteroides, Chlamydia, Gonococcus) leads to a Bartholin Cyst. When infected, it forms a rapidly expanding, exquisitely painful, fluctuant BARTHOLIN ABSCESS. Surgical Management of Choice: (1) MARSUPIALIZATION: A vertical incision is made along the mucosal surface of the abscess within the hymenal ring, the purulent contents are evacuated, and the cut edges of the cyst wall are everted and sutured to the adjacent vaginal mucosa with interrupted absorbable sutures, creating a permanent, open new drainage fistula that prevents re-accumulation while PRESERVING GLAND SECRETION; (2) WORD CATHETER PLACEMENT: A small rubber catheter with a balloon tip inflated with 3-5 mL of sterile saline, left in place for 4-6 weeks to allow epithelialization of a new tract. CRITICAL ONCOLOGICAL HAZARD: Simple incision and drainage (I&D) without marsupialization has a very high recurrence rate (> 20-30%). COMPLETE SURGICAL EXCISION of the Bartholin gland is STRICTLY RESERVED for chronic recurrent disease in uninflamed tissue, OR in ANY WOMAN AGED > 40 YEARS presenting with a new Bartholin mass to rule out BARTHOLIN GLAND CARCINOMA (adenocarcinoma or squamous cell carcinoma)!",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "CORRECT: Bartholin Glands (Greater Vestibular Glands) are paired mucus-secreting compound racemose glands located in the superficial perineal pouch at the 4 and 8 o'clock positions posterolaterally to the vaginal orifice. Obstruction of the Bartholin duct (due to inspissated mucus or infection: E. coli, Bacteroides, Chlamydia, Gonococcus) leads to a Bartholin Cyst. When infected, it forms a rapidly expanding, exquisitely painful, fluctuant BARTHOLIN ABSCESS. Surgical Management of Choice: (1) MARSUPIALIZATION: A vertical incision is made along the mucosal surface of the abscess within the hymenal ring, the purulent contents are evacuated, and the cut edges of the cyst wall are everted and sutured to the adjacent vaginal mucosa with interrupted absorbable sutures, creating a permanent, open new drainage fistula that prevents re-accumulation while PRESERVING GLAND SECRETION; (2) WORD CATHETER PLACEMENT: A small rubber catheter with a balloon tip inflated with 3-5 mL of sterile saline, left in place for 4-6 weeks to allow epithelialization of a new tract. CRITICAL ONCOLOGICAL HAZARD: Simple incision and drainage (I&D) without marsupialization has a very high recurrence rate (> 20-30%). COMPLETE SURGICAL EXCISION of the Bartholin gland is STRICTLY RESERVED for chronic recurrent disease in uninflamed tissue, OR in ANY WOMAN AGED > 40 YEARS presenting with a new Bartholin mass to rule out BARTHOLIN GLAND CARCINOMA (adenocarcinoma or squamous cell carcinoma)!",
                  "B": "INCORRECT: Complete surgical excision of the gland in the acute setting is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "C": "INCORRECT: Simple needle aspiration is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "D": "INCORRECT: Topical antibiotic ointment alone is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Bartholin Abscess Master Pearls: Location: Posterior introitus (4 and 8 o'clock). Procedure of Choice = MARSUPIALIZATION (preserves gland function!) or WORD CATHETER for 4 weeks. Simple I&D has high recurrence! RULE FOR OLDER WOMEN: Any new Bartholin mass in a woman > 40 years mandates BIOPSY / COMPLETE EXCISION to rule out Bartholin Gland Carcinoma!"
              },
