                  "A": "CORRECT: Lichen Sclerosus (Lichen Sclerosus et Atrophicus) is a chronic, progressive inflammatory mucocutaneous dermatosis with a predilection for the anogenital region in postmenopausal women. Hallmarks: Intense intractable pruritus (itching), burning, dyspareunia, porcelain-white atrophic patches with crinkled 'cigarette-paper' or parchment-like skin, architectural distortion (resorption and fusion of labia minora, buried clitoral hood, introital stenosis), and classic 'Figure-of-Eight' or 'Hourglass' perianal distribution. Histopathology: Hyperkeratosis, basal layer vacuolar degeneration, subepidermal band of dense hyalinized homogenized collagen, and deep band-like lymphocytic infiltrate. First-line Medical Treatment: ULTRA-POTENT TOPICAL CORTICOSTEROIDS (Clobetasol propionate 0.05% ointment for 3 months). ONCOLOGICAL SIGNIFICANCE: Lichen sclerosus is a premalignant condition carrying a 4-5% lifetime risk of progressing to INVASIVE VULVAR SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA via the HPV-negative, TP53-mutated Differentiated Vulvar Intraepithelial Neoplasia (dVIN) pathway! (In contrast, HPV-associated vulvar cancer occurs in younger women via the Usual VIN / uVIN pathway). ANY persistent ulcer, nodule, or raised non-healing plaque in lichen sclerosus mandates an immediate FULL-THICKNESS PUNCH BIOPSY (Keyes biopsy).",
                  "B": "INCORRECT: Lichen planus; Basal cell carcinoma is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "C": "INCORRECT: Extramammary Paget disease; Adenocarcinoma is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
