                "rationale": "WHO Classification divides Endometrial Hyperplasia into: (1) Hyperplasia Without Atypia: Risk of progression to cancer is < 1-3%; treated MEDICALLY with Progestins (Levonorgestrel IUD / Mirena is first-line, or oral medroxyprogesterone acetate); (2) Atypical Endometrial Hyperplasia / Endometrial Intraepithelial Neoplasia (EIN): Characterized by glandular crowding and cytologic atypia. Crucial Clinical Principle: EIN carries a 30 to 45% risk of CONCURRENT, CO-EXISTING INVASIVE ADENOCARCINOMA already present in the hysterectomy specimen, and a 30% lifetime risk of progressing to cancer! Therefore, the definitive treatment of choice in women who have completed childbearing is TOTAL HYSTERECTOMY with Bilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (frozen section evaluation of myometrial invasion). If fertility preservation is strongly desired: High-dose oral Megestrol acetate or Mirena IUD with mandatory endometrial biopsies every 3-6 months.",
