              "id": "PEDS_PRAC_U1_13",
              "question_type": "PRACTICE",
              "exam_source": "NEET PG High-Yield Practice (NBE / AIIMS Pattern)",
              "topic": "Vitamin A Deficiency & WHO Xerophthalmia Classification",
              "question": "According to the World Health Organization (WHO) xerophthalmia classification, what is the earliest clinical symptom of Vitamin A deficiency, and what is the stage designation for triangular, silvery-gray foamy plaques on the bulbar conjunctiva?",
              "options": {
                "A": "Night blindness (XN); Bitot's Spots (X1B)",
                "B": "Corneal ulceration (X3A); Keratomalacia (X3B)",
                "C": "Conjunctival xerosis (X1A); Xerophthalmic fundus (XF)",
                "D": "Corneal scar (XS); Night blindness (XN)"
              },
              "correct_option": "A",
              "explanation": {
                "rationale": "WHO Classification of Vitamin A Deficiency / Xerophthalmia: (1) XN: Night Blindness (Nyctalopia - earliest functional clinical symptom, due to impaired rhodopsin regeneration in retinal rods); (2) X1A: Conjunctival Xerosis (dry, dull, lusterless conjunctiva); (3) X1B: BITOT'S SPOTS (triangular, pearly-white / silvery-gray foamy plaques on bulbar conjunctiva near limbus, composed of keratinized epithelial debris and Corynebacterium xerosis); (4) X2: Corneal Xerosis (hazy, dry cornea without ulceration); (5) X3A: Corneal Ulceration / Keratomalacia involving < 1/3 of corneal surface; (6) X3B: Corneal Ulceration / Keratomalacia involving >= 1/3 of cornea (leads to perforation, iris prolapse, and permanent blindness); (7) XS: Corneal Scar (healed sequela); (8) XF: Xerophthalmic Fundus. National Vitamin A Prophylaxis Program (India): Routine oral doses starting at 9 months with Measles vaccine: 1st dose at 9 months = 100,000 IU (1 lakh IU); 2nd to 9th doses at 6-month intervals up to 5 years of age = 200,000 IU (2 lakh IU) each (Total 9 mega doses = 17 lakh IU). WHO Treatment of Active Xerophthalmia: 3 mega doses: Day 0, Day 1, and Day 14.",
                "distractor_analysis": {
                  "A": "CORRECT: WHO Classification of Vitamin A Deficiency / Xerophthalmia: (1) XN: Night Blindness (Nyctalopia - earliest functional clinical symptom, due to impaired rhodopsin regeneration in retinal rods); (2) X1A: Conjunctival Xerosis (dry, dull, lusterless conjunctiva); (3) X1B: BITOT'S SPOTS (triangular, pearly-white / silvery-gray foamy plaques on bulbar conjunctiva near limbus, composed of keratinized epithelial debris and Corynebacterium xerosis); (4) X2: Corneal Xerosis (hazy, dry cornea without ulceration); (5) X3A: Corneal Ulceration / Keratomalacia involving < 1/3 of corneal surface; (6) X3B: Corneal Ulceration / Keratomalacia involving >= 1/3 of cornea (leads to perforation, iris prolapse, and permanent blindness); (7) XS: Corneal Scar (healed sequela); (8) XF: Xerophthalmic Fundus. National Vitamin A Prophylaxis Program (India): Routine oral doses starting at 9 months with Measles vaccine: 1st dose at 9 months = 100,000 IU (1 lakh IU); 2nd to 9th doses at 6-month intervals up to 5 years of age = 200,000 IU (2 lakh IU) each (Total 9 mega doses = 17 lakh IU). WHO Treatment of Active Xerophthalmia: 3 mega doses: Day 0, Day 1, and Day 14.",
                  "B": "INCORRECT: Corneal ulceration (X3A); Keratomalacia (X3B) is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "C": "INCORRECT: Conjunctival xerosis (X1A); Xerophthalmic fundus (XF) is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                  "D": "INCORRECT: Corneal scar (XS); Night blindness (XN) is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
                },
                "high_yield_pearl": "Vitamin A Master Pearls: Earliest Symptom = NIGHT BLINDNESS (XN). Earliest Sign = Conjunctival Xerosis (X1A). BITOT'S SPOTS = X1B (foamy plaques on bulbar conjunctiva). Keratomalacia >= 1/3 cornea = X3B (medical emergency!). India Prophylaxis: Total 9 doses (1st dose 1 lakh IU at 9 mo, then 8 doses of 2 lakh IU every 6 mo up to 5 yrs = 17 lakh IU total)."
              },
              "tags": [
                "PEDS",
                "Vitamin A Deficiency & WHO Xerophthalmia Classification",
                "Unit 1",
                "High Yield Practice"
              ]
