                "rationale": "Ewing Sarcoma: The second most common primary malignant bone tumor of childhood and adolescence (peak age 10-15 years; male predominance). Neuroectodermal origin (Primitive Neuroectodermal Tumor / PNET family). Anatomical Location: Long bone DIAPHYSIS (femur, tibia, humerus) and flat bones of the pelvis (ilium). Clinical Presentation: Often mimics infection / osteomyelitis: localized bone pain, swelling, warmth, systemic low-grade fever, leukocytosis, and elevated ESR! Radiographic Hallmarks: (1) Permeative, poorly defined, destructive 'MOTH-EATEN' osteolytic lesion in the diaphysis, and (2) 'ONION-SKIN' / ONION-PEEL PERIOSTEAL REACTION (multiple concentric, lamellar, laminated layers of subperiosteal reactive bone) +/- Codman's triangle and hairpin soft-tissue mass. Histopathology Hallmark: Monotonous sheets of uniform SMALL ROUND BLUE CELLS with scant clear glycogen-rich cytoplasm (PAS-positive, diastase-sensitive), indistinct borders, round nuclei with fine chromatin, and Homer-Wright Pseudorosettes. Immunohistochemistry: STRONGLY AND DIFFUSELY POSITIVE FOR CD99 (MIC2 gene product; cell surface glycoprotein). PATHOGNOMONIC CYTOGENETICS: Chromosomal reciprocal translocation t(11;22)(q24;q12), creating the EWS-FLI1 chimeric transcription factor in 85-90% of cases (or t(21;22) EWS-ERG in 5-10%). Treatment: Highly sensitive to chemotherapy and radiation: Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy (VDC/IE: Vincristine, Doxorubicin, Cyclophosphamide alternating with Ifosfamide, Etoposide) -> Surgical resection -> Adjuvant chemo.",
