              "topic": "Vesiculobullous Disorders - Pemphigus vs Pemphigoid",
              "question": "A 48-year-old male presents with painful, non-healing oral erosions followed by widespread, fragile, flaccid bullae on normal skin that easily rupture, leaving tender weeping denuded areas. Gentle lateral traction on perilesional skin causes dislodgement of the epidermis (Nikolsky's Sign Positive). Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) of perilesional skin demonstrates an intercellular 'Fishnet' / 'Lace-Like' pattern of IgG and C3 throughout the epidermis. What is the molecular autoantibody target, and what histopathological hallmark is present?",
