                "rationale": "Normal Saline (0.9% NaCl) contains 154 mEq/L of Na+ and 154 mEq/L of Cl- with an osmolarity of 308 mOsm/L. Normal human plasma chloride is only 96-106 mEq/L. Infusion of large volumes of normal saline causes severe hyperchloremia, which reduces the strong ion difference (SID = [Na+] - [Cl-]), causing hydrogen ions to exit cells and renal bicarbonate wasting, producing a Dilutional Hyperchloremic Normal Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis. Furthermore, hyperchloremia induces renal afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction, decreasing renal blood flow and GFR. Balanced salt solutions (Ringer's Lactate, Plasmalyte) avoid this complication.",
